{
  "case_id": "2018-17-290",
  "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:05 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Merck Sharp & Dohme Corp.",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Respondents' express theory is that the FDA, fully informed of the risk of atypical femoral fractures, ignored that risk and its own statutory and regulatory responsibilities because it didn't like the way Merck phrased its proposed warning."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Merck Sharp & Dohme Corp.",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If a manufacturer, as Merck did here, informs the FDA of a possible risk and unsuccessfully asks to revise its label in light of that risk, then failure to warn claims based on that risk are preempted as a matter of law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Mr. Dvoretzky, let me give you a -- a -- a hypothetical because I think we can -- could all agree that if you had proposed a warning, let -- let me just say major -- major fractures versus stress fractures, and if you had proposed a warning that dealt with major fractures and the FDA had said no, we don't think that the literature supports that, then there's nothing you can do, and you should be immune from any suit."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus curiae)",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0000-0005",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STEWART: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: I'd like to begin by addressing an issue that both Justice Kagan and Justice Gorsuch have touched on, and I think it's important to distinguish between two potential types of confusion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Merck",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0001-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Merck explained at page 746 of the Joint Appendix in its proposal that it was using the term as an umbrella term to refer to fractures that could be partial or complete."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: But how are we to understand, Mr. Stewart, like what FDA understands at any given moment? In other words, what are we to look to when we decide whether it's impossible for Merck to change its label?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Merck",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. STEWART: Well, let me say two or three things about that. The first is you look first and foremost to the letter itself, and it would obviously have been better if the letter had stated without ambiguity the reason we are rejecting your proposed addition to the"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0001-0004",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If the FDA had said that clearly, Merck is not off the hook."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Dr. Lane",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0001-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I would ask you to look at the amicus brief by Dr. Lane in this case. Dr. Lane was a consultant for Merck in 2008 prior to Merck submitting its PAS. What Dr. Lane says is that surely by that time Merck would have had enough information to have prepared an adequate warning about these atypical femoral fractures."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Dr. Lane",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0002-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "if you look at Dr. Lane's amicus brief, they say this is a very specialized form of fracture that generally doesn't occur in the general population."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Frederick",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The first question is, what did the manufacturer know or should have known at a particular point in time? And then the next question is, what did the FDA understand about that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Frederick",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0002-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the budget of the FDA's drug safety division was less than the annual sales of this very drug."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "What Mr. Stewart cited was page 746 of the Joint Appendix, where Merck defines a stress fracture in this way: \"A stress fracture (also known as an insufficiency fracture) is defined as a partial or complete fracture occurring with either normal or increased activity but without an identifiable external traumatic event.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0002-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Frederick, let's be -- coming down to practical, okay?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: How about the manufacturer's duty to work with the FDA to ensure that the label is right and that it has all pertinent information to reconsider its initial decision?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 65,
        "start": 65
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Isn't that your burden?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Dvoretsky",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DVORETZKY: Justice Sotomayor, the PAS submission included 132 studies about the risk that Respondents say we should have warned about, the very same studies that Respondents themselves rely on that. All of that was before the FDA. The FDA isn't saying that it was in any way misled."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "FDA official",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0003-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 66,
        "start": 66
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the email that Mr. Frederick referred to, an FDA official told Merck to withdraw its request so that the FDA could close out this issue while it continued to study it and work with the FDA on -- work with Merck on language later if it determined that a warning was warranted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "FDA",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0003-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the data did not show an increase in this risk in women using this medication."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Dr. Lane",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0003-0005",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Dr. Lane himself in his amicus brief admitted that AFFs start as stress fractures."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court Clerk",
      "case_id": "2018-17-290",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "dd12b39ad5175049a737e55a121867f4c0917ed1b583aadf7b94e16d2a7991ba",
      "evidence_id": "ev-dd12b39ad517-0003-0006",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 68,
        "start": 68
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "(Whereupon, at 11:08 a.m., the case was submitted.)"
    },
